id: rust.tokio-select
kind: rust
title: tokio::select!
aliases: select, race futures, cancellation point
tags: async, concurrency, cancellation
questions:
  - how do you wait on several futures and stop waiting on the rest?
invariant: select! completes with one ready branch -- polling order unless you write `biased;` -- and drops the others, so every await inside it is a potential cancellation point.
answer_30s: select! races futures, resolves on the first completion and cancels the losers by dropping them, which makes it the standard tool for timeouts, shutdown and cancellation.
answer_2m: |
  The cancellation semantics are what interviewers probe: dropping a future
  stops it at its next await, so mid-way side effects remain. That is fine for
  request-scoped work and dangerous for work that must be atomic unless the step
  is idempotent or guarded. Branch order is randomised unless you write
  `biased;`, which matters when a shutdown branch must always win. Canonical
  uses: timeout (sleep branch), graceful shutdown (signal or cancellation
  token), and shutdown-aware loops (work versus token).
when_to_use:
  - timeouts, shutdown, racing a primary future against a control signal
avoid_when:
  - combining independent results you need all of (use join!)
minimal_example: |
  use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};

  tokio::select! {
      r = do_request() => tracing::info!(?r, "completed"),
      _ = sleep(Duration::from_millis(250)) => tracing::warn!("deadline exceeded"),
  }

  // A shutdown branch that must win:
  // tokio::select! { biased; _ = cancel.cancelled() => break, _ = do_work() => {} }
failure_modes:
  - a cancelled branch whose side effect already happened, so the operation runs twice
  - forgetting `biased;` and assuming a prioritised shutdown branch
  - holding a guard in a dropped branch, leaving state half-updated
related:
  - rust.cancellation
  - rust.timeout
  - rust.tokio-join
  - design.graceful-shutdown
sources:
  - https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/select
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/macro.select.html
